From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: shouchen <TheOneovb@petlover.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 10:20:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: sm8150-raphael: use the memory map ABL
 actually hands over

The board file carries a fixed /memory because sm8150.dtsi leaves the size
at 0 and expects the bootloader to fill it in. The ranges it carries are the
ones a UEFI image hands over, and under the stock ABL they are wrong: high
DRAM sits at 0x180000000, not 0x100000000.

Wrong here is not a matter of less RAM. The kernel is told about 3 GiB that
is not where it thinks, and not told about the 4 GiB that is -- its first
write into the phantom range faults, with no console up yet and no pstore to
catch it. What that looks like from outside: the bootloader shows its splash,
jumps, the screen goes black and the device comes back to fastboot a few
seconds later.

The replacement values are not derived or copied from another board. They are
read off this phone: /proc/device-tree/memory/reg under Android with root,
which is the tree ABL built.

Signed-off-by: shouchen <TheOneovb@petlover.com>
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8150-xiaomi-raphael.dts | 31 +++++++++++++---------
 1 file changed, 18 insertions(+), 13 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8150-xiaomi-raphael.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8150-xiaomi-raphael.dts
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8150-xiaomi-raphael.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8150-xiaomi-raphael.dts
@@ -38,22 +38,24 @@
 	qcom,board-id = <40 0>;
 
 	/*
-	 * Memory map as programmed by the bootloader.  sm8150.dtsi leaves the
-	 * size at 0 ("we expect the bootloader to fill in the size") and relies
-	 * on the board file for the real layout, so this is not optional: with
-	 * the placeholder reg the kernel gets zero bytes of RAM and dies before
-	 * it can even bring up USB.
+	 * Memory map as the ABL hands it over.
 	 *
-	 * The ranges are the ones the UEFI image hands over:
-	 *   0x80000000 + 0x3cc00000  low DRAM  (972 MiB)
-	 *   0x100000000 + 0xc0000000  high DRAM (3 GiB)
-	 *   0xc0000000 + 0x80000000   high DRAM (2 GiB)
+	 * These values are read off this phone under Android
+	 * (/proc/device-tree/memory/reg, root), so they are what ABL itself
+	 * puts there -- not what the UEFI image hands over, and that
+	 * difference is the whole point: UEFI puts high DRAM at 0x100000000,
+	 * ABL puts it at 0x180000000. A kernel told about memory that is not
+	 * there dies on its first write, with nothing on any console.
+	 *
+	 *   0x80000000  + 0x3bb00000   954 MiB
+	 *   0xc0000000  + 0xc0000000   3 GiB
+	 *   0x180000000 + 0x100000000  4 GiB
 	 */
 	memory@80000000 {
 		device_type = "memory";
-		reg = <0x00 0x80000000 0x00 0x3cc00000>,
-		      <0x01 0x40000000 0x00 0xc0000000>,
-		      <0x00 0xc0000000 0x00 0x80000000>;
+		reg = <0x00 0x80000000 0x00 0x3bb00000>,
+		      <0x00 0xc0000000 0x00 0xc0000000>,
+		      <0x01 0x80000000 0x01 0x00000000>;
 	};
 
 	aliases {
